1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,143
Coach: Combining contributions does not mean giving every suggestion its own full slot.

2
00:00:05,143 --> 00:00:09,429
Coach: Look for a connection between the jobs they could do.

3
00:00:09,429 --> 00:00:13,286
Coach: An example might prepare people for a pair task;

4
00:00:13,286 --> 00:00:18,429
Coach: sharing might let the group compare something that came from that task.

5
00:00:18,429 --> 00:00:24,000
Coach: That is a sequence with a reason, rather than a list of favourites.

6
00:00:24,350 --> 00:00:27,350
Coach: The two aims still govern the combination.

7
00:00:27,350 --> 00:00:31,636
Coach: Newcomers should have an opportunity to try discussing a text,

8
00:00:31,636 --> 00:00:36,778
Coach: and there should be a way to learn about the weekly club.

9
00:00:36,778 --> 00:00:43,207
Coach: A plan can be neat and still miss one aim if you remove a contribution

10
00:00:43,207 --> 00:00:46,207
Coach: without replacing the job it was doing.

11
00:00:46,557 --> 00:00:48,700
Coach: Keep the available resources specific.

12
00:00:48,700 --> 00:00:52,557
Coach: The group has the supplied text and information sheet,

13
00:00:52,557 --> 00:00:55,986
Coach: with paper and pens in the main room.

14
00:00:55,986 --> 00:01:00,272
Coach: Do not improve the candidate by quietly adding a video,

15
00:01:00,272 --> 00:01:04,986
Coach: an outside room or equipment that the task has not provided.

16
00:01:13,336 --> 00:01:16,336
Coach: Thirty minutes is the total activity time.

17
00:01:16,336 --> 00:01:20,622
Coach: At least five of those minutes must remain for questions.

18
00:01:20,622 --> 00:01:24,479
Coach: That leaves at most twenty-five for everything else,

19
00:01:24,479 --> 00:01:27,907
Coach: including transitions, and less if questions receive longer.

20
00:01:27,907 --> 00:01:34,336
Coach: Twenty-five is a limit to respect, not a schedule the group has already chosen.

21
00:01:34,686 --> 00:01:38,543
Coach: An attractive combination may also depend on a person

22
00:01:38,543 --> 00:01:40,686
Coach: or an unanswered practical question.

23
00:01:40,686 --> 00:01:44,115
Coach: Here, someone would have to give the example.

24
00:01:44,115 --> 00:01:49,257
Coach: We have not established who is willing or what preparation they need.

25
00:01:49,257 --> 00:01:55,686
Coach: Keep that check attached to the stage instead of hiding it behind the word demonstration.

26
00:01:56,036 --> 00:02:01,607
Coach: This does not mean you must solve every detail before suggesting a connection.

27
00:02:01,607 --> 00:02:07,179
Coach: You can propose a candidate and name the checks needed to judge it.

28
00:02:07,179 --> 00:02:10,179
Coach: The difference is between a plausible direction

29
00:02:10,179 --> 00:02:14,893
Coach: and a plan that has already been timed, resourced and adopted.

30
00:02:15,243 --> 00:02:17,814
Coach: Some ideas cannot be combined unchanged.

31
00:02:17,814 --> 00:02:20,814
Coach: They may compete for the same minutes,

32
00:02:20,814 --> 00:02:24,243
Coach: rely on different assumptions, or duplicate a job.

33
00:02:24,243 --> 00:02:30,672
Coach: Explain the clash instead of treating the person who offered an idea as the problem.

34
00:02:30,672 --> 00:02:36,243
Coach: An element can be shortened, linked differently or set aside with a reason.

35
00:02:36,593 --> 00:02:38,307
Coach: Now compare three combinations.

36
00:02:38,307 --> 00:02:43,022
Coach: Keep the same goals and resource limits beside all of them.

37
00:02:43,022 --> 00:02:48,165
Coach: Do not choose the longest list because it seems to respect everyone,

38
00:02:48,165 --> 00:02:52,879
Coach: or the shortest list just because it is easy to describe.

39
00:02:53,229 --> 00:02:57,086
Coach: Look for the candidate that makes a useful connection

40
00:02:57,086 --> 00:03:00,086
Coach: without pretending the remaining checks are complete.

41
00:03:00,086 --> 00:03:04,801
Coach: Its next step should be something the group can actually discuss,

42
00:03:04,801 --> 00:03:08,658
Coach: rather than an announcement that the planning is finished.

43
00:03:17,008 --> 00:03:21,722
You: We could use one brief example to open the pair task,

44
00:03:21,722 --> 00:03:27,294
You: then share an idea and keep at least five minutes for club questions.

45
00:03:27,294 --> 00:03:32,865
You: We still need to check the timing and who would give the example.

46
00:03:33,215 --> 00:03:39,644
Maya: I can support that connection if the example leaves enough room for the pair discussion.

47
00:03:39,994 --> 00:03:44,708
Coach: This candidate gives the contributions related jobs and protects question time.

48
00:03:44,708 --> 00:03:46,423
Coach: Maya's support remains conditional.

49
00:03:46,423 --> 00:03:51,137
Coach: The durations and the person giving the example still need checking,

50
00:03:51,137 --> 00:03:58,423
Coach: so the board should show a candidate to develop rather than a finished or unanimously accepted plan.

51
00:03:58,773 --> 00:04:03,059
Coach: Whatever you chose, inspect both the connection and its limits.

52
00:04:03,059 --> 00:04:07,773
Coach: The parts need related purposes, the whole must respect the task,

53
00:04:07,773 --> 00:04:10,344
Coach: and unresolved checks should remain visible.

54
00:04:10,344 --> 00:04:13,344
Coach: Those tests help you develop a proposal;

55
00:04:13,344 --> 00:04:19,773
Coach: they do not prove how visitors will respond or whether the group will accept it.

56
00:04:32,123 --> 00:04:38,552
Coach: A workable proposal is not a promise to keep every element at its original size.

57
00:04:38,552 --> 00:04:42,837
Coach: If one part grows, something else may have to change.

58
00:04:42,837 --> 00:04:48,837
Coach: Make that consequence explicit so the group can compare a real trade-off rather

59
00:04:48,837 --> 00:04:50,980
Coach: than approve several incompatible promises.

60
00:04:51,330 --> 00:04:57,330
Coach: The next step is to explain why a particular connection fits the shared aims,

61
00:04:57,330 --> 00:04:59,473
Coach: and what it gives up.

62
00:04:59,473 --> 00:05:04,187
Coach: That lets others question your reasoning or offer a better arrangement

63
00:05:04,187 --> 00:05:08,473
Coach: without first having to undo an exaggerated claim of agreement.
